    "message": "I am thinking...: Now I understand the vulnerability! The commit `386a1ac` removed `bIgnoreData`. Before this commit, when `bIgnoreData` was true (during seek operations), the code would NOT advance the `data` pointer:\n\n```cpp\nif (!d->bIgnoreData) {  // Move on in the output buffer\n    data += outReceived;\n    availOut = maxlen - dataReceived;\n} else if (maxlen - dataReceived < outBufferSize) {\n    availOut = maxlen - dataReceived;\n}\n```\n\nWhen `bIgnoreData` was true, data would stay at the same position, but `outBufferSize` was limited to `3 * BUFFER_SIZE`. The issue was that when seeking, the output was being written to a dummy buffer, but the data pointer wasn't being advanced, meaning subsequent decompression calls would write past the end of the dummy buffer.\n\nWait no, re-reading more carefully:\n\nWhen `bIgnoreData` was true:\n- `outBufferSize = qMin(maxlen, static_cast<qint64>(3 * BUFFER_SIZE))`\n- `outBufferSize -= dataReceived` \n- The output buffer was set as: `filter->setOutBuffer(data, outBufferSize)`\n- After each loop iteration: data was NOT advanced (stays at same spot)\n- But `availOut` would be updated: `availOut = maxlen - dataReceived` when remaining < outBufferSize\n\nThe critical issue: `maxlen` could be much larger than `3 * BUFFER_SIZE` (which is 24KB). When seeking, `bytesToRead` could be large, and `read(dummy.data(), bytesToRead)` is called where `dummy` is `qMin(bytesToRead, 3 * BUFFER_SIZE)` = at most 24KB.\n\nSo `read()` is called with `maxlen = bytesToRead` but the actual buffer is only `3 * BUFFER_SIZE`. Inside `readData`, with `bIgnoreData = true`:\n- `outBufferSize = qMin(maxlen, 3 * BUFFER_SIZE)` caps at 24KB\n- But wait, `maxlen` is the parameter to readData, which should be the size of the buffer... \n\nActually, wait. `read(dummy.data(), bytesToRead)` calls QIODevice::read which may call readData multiple times or buffer data internally. The key insight from the commit message is:\n\n\"The problem is t